-------------------------------------------------------------------
Tribes 2 Voice Over IP - HOWTO

First draft by Sam Lantinga, April 19, 2001


Part 1:  Equipment

The first thing you need is a sound card that supports full duplex
audio, in other words, it can both play and record at the same time.
We have tested this with the following sound cards and chipsets:
        SoundBlaster Live!
        ES1370-based cards
If you get this working with other sound cards, please let us know!
Your sound driver must support the OSS API for proper functionality.

You also need a microphone.  You can get a nice headset with both
microphone and and headphones fairly inexpensively.  I recommend
this as it's more comfortable than leaning into a standalone mike.

Plug the microphone into the microphone input jack on your sound card.


Part 2:  Verifying the equipment

In order to test your microphone setup, try using a standalone application
to record your voice.  If this works while playing sound, then you are
well on your way to having Voice Over IP in Tribes 2.  If this doesn't
work, there are a couple things you can try:

1. Is the microphone enabled for input?  Is the record volume high enough?
   Use a standalone mixer program like xmixer to see whether or not recording
   is enabled on the microphone input.  Make sure the volume is turned high
   enough.  I usually set the microphone volume at the maximum.

2. Do you have the microphone plugged into the right jack?
   Many times it's nearly impossible to tell whether or not you have the
   right jack.  Once I have the mixer settings right, I have a crude method
   of telling when I have the right jack.  I cat /dev/dsp, plug the mike
   into the jack I want to try, and then blow on it.  I should see lots of
   garbage on the screen changing as I blow on the microphone.  Then I hit
   Ctrl-C to stop seeing the raw microphone data on the screen.


Part 3:  Testing voice in the game

When you launch the game, you can verify that voice recording works by
going to the voice section of the "Settings" menu, and pressing the test
voice button.  The game will start recording for 5 seconds, and then play
it back.  If this works, then the physical portion of the setup is 
complete, and you should be able to use voice in the game.


Part 4:  Using voice in the game

Voice communications in Tribes 2 works a little like a CB radio.  You
can only speak with your teammates, and in general only one person can
be talking at a time.  To activate voice communications, press and hold
the 'x' key while you say something.  There is a five second limit on
the amount you can say.  When you release the 'x' key, it is broadcast
to all of your teammates who you have selected for voice communication.
You can select which of your teammates you can hear and can hear you, by
hitting escape while in the game and right clicking on their name in the
lobby screen.  You'll get a menu of things you can do, and among them are
various voice related options.

If your voice has a lot of static and "breaking up", make sure that you
have the microphone a few inches away from your mouth, and make sure that
the microphone boost in the voice option menu isn't set too high.

The best way to use voice in the game is to communicate with quick simple
comments so everyone can clearly understand you.  Long complex communication
is still better done by typing at the global chat window (pressing 'u').


Part 5:  Codecs and how they affect you

In the voice section of the settings menu, you can adjust the codec that
is used for compressing the voice and sending it over the network.  If
you've checked everything listed above, and you still can't hear each
other at all, it's possible that you and your teammate have mismatching
voice codecs.  The Linux version of the game has only the high quality,
high bandwidth GSM codec, so all Linux players will be able to talk to
each other seamlessly.  For Windows players to hear Linux players, they
must set the receiving codec list to include the GSM codec.  For Windows
players to speak to Linux players, they must use the GSM codec to send
voice to other players.

As an interesting historical note, the GSM codec used in Tribes 2 is a
free implementation of the same voice compression technology used in 
digital phones in Europe.  The difference in voice quality is caused
by techniques used by Tribes 2 to reduce the bandwidth requirements of
GSM to work well with a fast-paced online game like Tribes 2.


Conclusion:

Getting voice communication set up the first time takes a little bit of
effort.  However, once you start taunting and cheering with your teammates,
I think you'll find it's really worth it. :)

Enjoy!
	-Sam Lantinga, Lead Programmer, Loki Software, Inc.
